    <item>
      <title>Linus Torvalds on AI use in coding</title>
      <link>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/26/lf-git-repositories/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:05:18 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/26/lf-git-repositories/</guid>
      <description>&lt;article class=&#34;cutting cutting-shared&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-meta&#34; data-pagefind-ignore&gt; &lt;time class=&#34;cutting-date&#34; datetime=&#34;2026-10-26&#34;&gt;26 Oct 2026&lt;/time&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-comment&#34;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Linus Torvalds with some wise words, in his own inimitable style :D&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-card&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;h2 class=&#34;cutting-title&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/CAHk-=whTs09_ozN+e09GU68N1wPP56MUzDjrg9rnKxQUa0A_4Q@mail.gmail.com/&#34;&gt;Linus Torvalds on AI use in coding&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;p class=&#34;cutting-source&#34;&gt;lore.kernel.org&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;figure class=&#34;cutting-quote&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;blockquote cite=&#34;https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/CAHk-=whTs09_ozN&amp;#43;e09GU68N1wPP56MUzDjrg9rnKxQUa0A_4Q@mail.gmail.com/&#34;&gt;&lt;p&gt;I don&amp;rsquo;t understand this whole idiotic concept of &amp;ldquo;don&amp;rsquo;t use AI for X&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here&amp;rsquo;s a new rule just for you: don&amp;rsquo;t use compilers for any of your kernel submissions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Because if you can&amp;rsquo;t show that you really understand what the machine is actually doing by writing your code in assembler, then I don&amp;rsquo;t want to see your code contribution. You clearly aren&amp;rsquo;t competent if you can&amp;rsquo;t write it that way.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Practitioner Voice</title>
      <link>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/21/practitioner-voice/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 15:27:06 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/21/practitioner-voice/</guid>
      <description>&lt;article class=&#34;cutting cutting-shared&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-meta&#34; data-pagefind-ignore&gt; &lt;time class=&#34;cutting-date&#34; datetime=&#34;2026-10-21&#34;&gt;21 Oct 2026&lt;/time&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-card&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;h2 class=&#34;cutting-title&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://martinfowler.com/articles/practitioner-voice.html&#34;&gt;Practitioner Voice&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;p class=&#34;cutting-source&#34;&gt;Jim Highsmith · martinfowler.com&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;figure class=&#34;cutting-quote&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;blockquote cite=&#34;https://martinfowler.com/articles/practitioner-voice.html&#34;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Academic writing hides behind rigor. Thought leadership hides behind polish. Now a third option has arrived — LLM Voice — that doesn&amp;rsquo;t require hiding at all, because there&amp;rsquo;s no one there.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Practitioner authority comes from having been in the situation, not as a researcher studying it, but as a person responsible for the outcome.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Pluralistic: Itch scratching</title>
      <link>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/20/pluralistic-itch-scratching/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:27:04 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/20/pluralistic-itch-scratching/</guid>
      <description>&lt;article class=&#34;cutting cutting-shared&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-meta&#34; data-pagefind-ignore&gt; &lt;time class=&#34;cutting-date&#34; datetime=&#34;2026-10-20&#34;&gt;20 Oct 2026&lt;/time&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-card&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;h2 class=&#34;cutting-title&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://pluralistic.net/2026/09/25/other-people/&#34;&gt;Pluralistic: Itch scratching&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;p class=&#34;cutting-source&#34;&gt;Cory Doctorow · pluralistic.net&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;figure class=&#34;cutting-quote&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;blockquote cite=&#34;https://pluralistic.net/2026/09/25/other-people/&#34;&gt;&lt;p&gt;I think that so much of the outrage about slop code – floods of garbagey pull requests and bug reports – is the result of the collapse of this dynamic. Slop&amp;rsquo;s not merely annoying or time-wasting: it&amp;rsquo;s a betrayal of the love and care that goes into writing and maintaining code for others.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Five Camps of Data Modeling (and Which One You’re Stuck In)</title>
      <link>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/19/the-five-camps-of-data-modeling-and-which-one-youre-stuck-in/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:27:04 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://rmoff.net/cuttings/2026/10/19/the-five-camps-of-data-modeling-and-which-one-youre-stuck-in/</guid>
      <description>&lt;article class=&#34;cutting cutting-shared&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-meta&#34; data-pagefind-ignore&gt; &lt;time class=&#34;cutting-date&#34; datetime=&#34;2026-10-19&#34;&gt;19 Oct 2026&lt;/time&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;div class=&#34;cutting-card&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;h2 class=&#34;cutting-title&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://practicaldatamodeling.substack.com/p/the-five-camps-of-data-modeling-and&#34;&gt;The Five Camps of Data Modeling (and Which One You’re Stuck In)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;p class=&#34;cutting-source&#34;&gt;Joe Reis · practicaldatamodeling.substack.com&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;figure class=&#34;cutting-quote&#34;&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;blockquote cite=&#34;https://practicaldatamodeling.substack.com/p/the-five-camps-of-data-modeling-and&#34;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Practitioners rarely look beyond their niche. Data engineers who’ve never designed a normalized table. App devs who’ve never heard of slowly changing dimensions. ML engineers who’ve never modeled a core business domain. Ontologists who’ve never shipped a production pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&#x9;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#x9;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
